/* Fixes and jobs written by Claude for Connor to review in the hub.
   Claude adds to this file and pushes it to GitHub. Pull in GitHub Desktop to see new ones.
   Nothing here touches the website until Connor approves it in the Fix queue.

   elementor_tidy: removes the space or line break before punctuation in one element (element: Elementor id).
   elementor_layout: changes one element's margin, padding or max width (element, set, clearNeg). Sides not named are kept.
   needsEdit: true on a text fix means Connor must change the wording before it will push.
   elementor_style: changes how one image widget is displayed (find: image file name, set: {object-fit, height...}). Undo restores.
   page_build: copies an existing Elementor page's design into a NEW draft, swaps in new text (edits: [{op: set|swap|remove, find, replace, level}]),
     returns a preview link. Set ready: true once the text is written.
   Fix types: page_build, meta_title, meta_description, content_replace, new_draft, elementor_set, elementor_swap, elementor_remove,
   set_status (the hub pushes these)
   and manual (step-by-step fixes you tick off). priority: critical, high, medium, low.
   category: Content, Structure, Indexing, AI search.
   pageId 0 means the hub finds the page from its address when you push.
   progress: {update, nextDate, note} puts a fix In progress once per update stamp (bump the stamp to apply again).
   tracks: [{name, status, next, log: [{date, text}]}] shows a Progress log with one tab per vendor or strand.
     status: To do, Waiting on them, Done, Not possible. A new progress stamp lets these statuses override Connor's.
   reopen + reopenNote: when a fix Claude wrote went wrong or didn't work, Claude MUST send it back from Done to
     Waiting for you in the same release: set reopen to a new stamp (date-time) and reopenNote to why it's back.
     Connor should never have to move a failed fix back himself. Its last push stays in the card's history.
   page_restore: a broken page. The card lists Elementor's own saved versions to restore from.
   page_check: an automatic check the hub runs itself (pages, kinds, okKind, needs, widths). Use this instead of
     asking Connor to look at pages: anything that can be measured is checked by the hub, not by hand.
   Human checks: anything Claude thinks a person should look at (how a page reads, feels or works for a visitor,
     not just how Google sees it) goes into Your jobs as a "Human check" job, with what to look at and why.
   Size: "small" pushes after one approval. "big" asks for a second confirmation. */

