  "proposed": "Heading: Phone systems for law firms: common questions\n\nQ: What does a law firm need from its phone system?\nA: Calls that reach the right fee earner first time, and recordings you can find months later if a matter is disputed. Most firms we support now run on a cloud platform such as 8x8, Zoom Phone or Microsoft Teams calling. A solicitor can take a client's call on a laptop or mobile, and the client still sees the firm's number.\n\nQ: Can calls with clients be recorded and stored securely?\nA: Yes. Cloud platforms record calls automatically and keep them behind role-based access, so only the people you choose can play them back. 8x8 offers storage plans with retention of up to ten years, which suits matters that run long or get reopened. Before you switch recording on, update your privacy notice and tell callers the call may be recorded.\n\nQ: Can fee earners work from home without clients noticing?\nA: Yes. The app on a laptop or mobile carries the same direct number and extension as the desk phone. Calls can be transferred to a colleague or a secretary exactly as they would be in the office, and reception can see who is free before putting a client through.\n\nQ: Does it work with Microsoft Teams?\nA: Yes, if your firm already lives in Teams. 8x8 and Zoom Phone both integrate with it, and Gamma connects external phone lines straight into Teams through Direct Routing or Operator Connect. You keep one app for chat, meetings and client calls.\n\nQ: Can the phone system link to our case management or CRM software?\nA: Often. 8x8 connects to Salesforce and Microsoft Dynamics out of the box, and some practice management systems can open a client's record when their number rings in. Tell us which system you use and we will check what is possible before you commit to anything.\n\nQ: We still run an older Avaya system. Should we move?\nA: Not necessarily straight away. Plenty of firms run Avaya reliably, including our customer Barwells Solicitors. Two things are worth knowing, though. Openreach closes the old copper phone network on 31 January 2027, so any ISDN or analogue lines must move before then. And Avaya has been focusing on its largest enterprise customers, which can mean slower support for smaller firms. We can keep your Avaya system running while you plan the move.",
