  "proposed": "Heading: Police communications: common questions\n\nQ: Are your engineers vetted to work on police sites?\nA: Yes. Our engineers hold NPPV3 (Non-Police Personnel Vetting level 3) clearance. That is the level forces normally ask for when contractors work in police buildings or on systems that carry sensitive information.\n\nQ: Which police forces do you work with?\nA: We work with Warwickshire Police, and with Sussex and Surrey Police on integrated communications for control rooms and frontline officers. We also support Sussex Police's Avaya telephony.\n\nQ: Can you support a force still running Avaya or Mitel?\nA: Yes. We maintain existing Avaya estates and we are a Mitel partner. Many forces keep their current system running safely while a replacement is procured, and we can support both sides of that change so nothing drops in between.\n\nQ: What happens to police phone lines when ISDN closes?\nA: Openreach closes the old copper network on 31 January 2027. Lines that have not moved by then fall back to a temporary emergency voice service, which Openreach describes as limited and more expensive. We audit every line a force uses, including alarm and lift lines at remote stations, and move each one to a digital service before the deadline.\n\nQ: How do forces usually buy from you?\nA: Most forces buy through a framework or a further competition run by their procurement team. Tell us the route your force prefers and we will confirm whether we can take part through it.\n\nQ: Can officers' mobiles use the same phone system as the station?\nA: Yes. We supply business mobiles as well as fixed lines, and the phone system's app runs on a work mobile. An officer's direct number then follows them, whether they are at a desk or out on patrol.",
