  "proposed": "Heading: Healthcare phone systems: common questions\n\nQ: Who do you work with in healthcare?\nA: Private clinics, and the companies that supply healthcare, from medical manufacturers to health cash plan providers. Our customers include Kimal Plc, which makes procedure packs and vascular access devices for hospitals, and Sovereign Health Care. NHS GP practices buy cloud telephony through NHS England's own framework, so they are not who this page is for.\n\nQ: How can we cut the queue on our booking line at peak times?\nA: Use call queues that tell callers their position, plus a call-back option so nobody has to hold. Routing callers by what they need, such as new bookings or test results, sends each call to the right team first time. 8x8's contact centre includes queue call-back as standard.\n\nQ: Can the phone system work with our patient record system?\nA: In many cases, yes. A caller's number can open their record on screen before the call is answered, which saves time and cuts mistakes. Integration depends on the system you use, so we check yours before you commit to anything.\n\nQ: Is it safe to record calls with patients?\nA: Yes, when it is set up properly. Recordings are encrypted and kept behind role-based access, and you choose how long they are kept. Patients must be told calls may be recorded, and your privacy notice should explain why and for how long.\n\nQ: How are urgent and out-of-hours calls handled?\nA: Time-based rules send calls to an on-call clinician's mobile, a recorded message or an answering service once the clinic closes. The rules change themselves at set times, so nobody has to remember to switch anything over.\n\nQ: Will moving to a cloud system disrupt our clinics?\nA: It should not. We plan the switch outside clinic hours, move your existing numbers across, and test every line and handset before staff arrive. Patients keep dialling the same numbers throughout.",
