  "proposed": "Heading: School phone systems: common questions\n\nQ: What phone system suits a school with several buildings?\nA: One system that covers every building, so a call can be transferred from reception to a science block office as easily as to the desk next door. Many of our schools run 3CX, including Ifield Community College in Crawley and Glebelands School in Cranleigh, both spread across multiple campus buildings.\n\nQ: How is 3CX priced for a school?\nA: 3CX is licensed by the number of calls that can happen at the same time, not by the number of staff. A school with 120 staff that rarely has more than 16 calls at once pays for 16. That usually works out cheaper than paying for every member of staff.\n\nQ: Can parents leave absence messages that reach the office?\nA: Yes. An absence line can record messages and email them to the attendance team, so they are picked up even when the office is busy at 8.30am. Messages can be sorted by year group if that suits how your school works.\n\nQ: Will 999 calls show the right address?\nA: They must. Ofcom rules require emergency calls to carry an accurate location. On a modern system each building and handset is registered to the correct address, and we check this as part of every school install.\n\nQ: When do you install a new system?\nA: In the holidays or a half term, so lessons are never disturbed. Numbers are moved across and every handset is tested before staff return.\n\nQ: What happens to our old phone lines in 2027?\nA: Openreach closes the old copper phone network on 31 January 2027. Schools still on ISDN or analogue lines, including fire alarm and lift lines, need to move to digital services before then. We check every line on site, so nothing is missed.",
