  "proposed": "Heading: Hotel and hospitality phone systems: common questions\n\nQ: Can the phone system link to our property management system?\nA: Yes. 3CX's hotel features connect to property management systems such as Oracle's Micros Fidelio. Checking a guest in opens their room phone for outside calls, and checking out clears voicemail and closes the line. Calls are logged per room, so charges can go straight onto the guest's bill.\n\nQ: Do we have to replace every room phone?\nA: Not usually. Existing room phones can often stay connected through adapters, so you only replace the handsets at reception and in the back office. That keeps the cost of moving down, especially in larger hotels.\n\nQ: Can guests book wake-up calls without calling reception?\nA: Yes. Guests set a wake-up call from the room phone by following a short recorded menu, and reception can still book or change one from their screen.\n\nQ: How do we make sure booking calls are answered when the front desk is busy?\nA: Ring groups and overflow rules send unanswered calls to the back office or a reservations team after a few rings. Out of hours, an AI receptionist can take booking enquiries and pass the details on, rather than leaving the call to ring out.\n\nQ: Who do you work with in hospitality?\nA: Our customers include Stylotel, a modern hotel in central London, and Glenshee Ski Centre in the Cairngorms, one of the largest snowsport areas in the UK. Sites like Glenshee also show why reliable connectivity matters as much as the phones themselves.\n\nQ: What changes when the old phone network closes?\nA: Openreach closes the copper network on 31 January 2027. Room phones, lift lines and payment terminals still running on analogue lines need checking before then, so nothing stops working on the day.",
