  "proposed": "Heading: Charity phone systems: common questions\n\nQ: How can a charity keep phone costs down?\nA: Pay only for the people who need a line, and run the phone system as an app on laptops and mobiles you already own. For very small teams, 3CX has a free plan for up to ten users; you pay only for calls and numbers. Larger charities usually save by replacing several old contracts with one.\n\nQ: Can volunteers answer our helpline from home?\nA: Yes. Volunteers sign in to an app on a laptop or mobile, and the helpline number is shown to callers, never the volunteer's own. Supervisors can see who is available and step in on difficult calls.\n\nQ: Can we report on helpline calls for funders and trustees?\nA: Yes. Call reports show how many calls came in, how many were answered and how long people waited. Funders and trustees increasingly ask for this, and it helps when you are making the case for more volunteers or extra opening hours.\n\nQ: How do we keep supporter and donor data safe?\nA: Start with Cyber Essentials, the government-backed scheme from the National Cyber Security Centre, and switch on multi-factor authentication for every cloud account. Under the scheme's April 2026 rules, failing to use multi-factor authentication where it is available is an automatic fail.\n\nQ: Which charities do you work with?\nA: Our customers include Mind, Children With Cancer UK and Charities Trust.\n\nQ: Can you help us move without disrupting our services?\nA: Yes. We plan the switch around your opening hours and move your existing numbers across. Every line is tested before the old system is turned off, so supporters calling in never notice the change.",
