  "proposed": "Heading: Cyber security: common questions\n\nQ: What is Cyber Essentials, and do we need it?\nA: Cyber Essentials is the government-backed scheme run with the National Cyber Security Centre. It checks five basic controls that stop the most common attacks. It is required for many central government contracts, and more private customers now ask for it before they sign.\n\nQ: What changed in Cyber Essentials in April 2026?\nA: The rules got stricter for assessments started after 26 April 2026. Multi-factor authentication is now mandatory on every cloud service that offers it, and high-risk or critical security updates must be installed within 14 days. Missing either is an automatic fail.\n\nQ: Is multi-factor authentication really worth the hassle?\nA: Yes. Microsoft has said multi-factor authentication blocks over 99.9% of account compromise attacks. It adds a few seconds to signing in, and it stops one leaked password opening your email and finance systems.\n\nQ: Can a phone system be hacked?\nA: Yes. The most common attack is toll fraud, where criminals break into a phone system and run up expensive international calls, often over a weekend. Strong passwords and barring calls to countries you never ring stop most of it. Keep the system updated too.\n\nQ: Doesn't Microsoft 365 back up our data already?\nA: Not in the way most people assume. Microsoft keeps its service running, but protecting your data from deletion or ransomware is your responsibility. A separate backup lets you restore a mailbox or folder in minutes.\n\nQ: Where should a small business start?\nA: Turn on multi-factor authentication everywhere and make sure updates install automatically. Then check your backups actually restore, and work towards Cyber Essentials. We set up the protection and help you through the assessment.",
