  "proposed": "Heading: Common questions about Avaya\n\nQ: Is Avaya IP Office still supported?\nA: Current releases are. IP Office R12.2 is the latest release and has full support. R11.1 mainstream support ended on 13 October 2026, and older releases can no longer get new licences or Avaya software fixes. Our <a href=\"https://westpiertech.com/avaya-ip-office-end-of-sale-and-support/\">IP Office end of sale guide</a> has the dates for each release.\n\nQ: What should we do if our IP Office is on an old release?\nA: There are two sensible routes. Upgrade to R12.2, often on your existing hardware if it's compatible, or plan a staged move to a cloud system such as 8x8, Gamma or Zoom Phone. We can help with either.\n\nQ: Can you support an Avaya system that's out of support?\nA: Yes. We provide third-party support and maintenance for older IP Office systems, including IP500 V2 and Server Edition. Avaya won't issue software fixes for those releases, but we keep the system running and fix faults.\n\nQ: Do we have to replace Avaya all at once to move to the cloud?\nA: No. You can move in stages, keeping Avaya where it works and moving sites or teams over one at a time. Each stage is tested with a rollback plan before we go further.\n\nQ: Is Avaya still a good choice for contact centres?\nA: For large, complex contact centres it often is, especially where there are bespoke integrations or strict security rules. Smaller teams usually get better value from a cloud contact centre. Read <a href=\"https://westpiertech.com/is-avaya-really-dead-in-2026-or-just-misunderstood/\">is Avaya really dead in 2026?</a> for more.",
  "why": "AI tools and Google's AI Overviews pull answers from clear question-and-answer content. Approving adds a heading and an accordion just above “Why switch to West Pier”. Elementor's FAQ schema is switched on, but it does little now: Google stopped showing FAQ rich results in May 2026, so the value is the answers themselves. Check the answers about what West Pier does are right before you approve. Push again later to replace the block, and Undo takes it off."
